Autonomous vehicles are expected to share the road with manually driven cars. The introduction of autonomous technologies on a larger scale appears to be most realistic in the context of road freight transport. The integration of these technologies will yield several advantages, including the streamlining of the entire transport process and the resolution of issues pertaining to driver shortages. In autonomous driving mode, the drivers will have the opportunity to allocate their time to other activities. This prompts the question of suitable activities and their potential impact on road traffic safety in a broader context. This paper evaluates the impact of non-driving-related tasks on driving performance after a takeover request. To this end, a study was conducted involving 31 participants who engaged with a truck and bus simulator. Six different activities in three scenarios were tested. The performance of the drivers is evaluated using various measures, including takeover time, longitudinal and lateral changes (average speed and distance of the vehicle to the centre of the lane) and a situational awareness assessment. The utilisation of these metrics facilitates the discernment of risky activities from their safe counterparts. The simulator method was supplemented with several other methods (e.g., SAGAT).
1.1 Level 3 (conditional automation) and takeover request SAE International [4] has delineated six levels of automated driving: no automation (L0), vehicles with driver assistance (L1), partial driving automation (L2), conditional automation (L3), high automation (L4) and full automation (L5) [4]. The present study focuses on conditionally automated driving - Level 3. The third level of automated driving enables the system to assume complete control of the steering in specified conditions [4]. Vehicles at L3 are capable of independent autonomous driving without the driver’s participation. However, it is important to note that there may be situations in which the system is unable to handle these tasks independently. In such cases, a takeover request (TOR) is initiated, which involves a transition from autonomous to manual steering modes. This request is made to the driver, who is then responsible for taking control of the vehicle from the autonomous mode. This transition is
referred to as takeover performance or fallback performance (see Fig. 1). In summary, the drivers of L3 vehicles are not required to maintain manual control of the steering wheel or to monitor the surrounding traffic. While not actively driving, they are required to maintain situational awareness to promptly take over steering responsibilities, assess the prevailing traffic conditions, and react accordingly. A takeover process is comprised of multiple stages. The initial stage is the perception of the takeover request, which can be facilitated through visual, auditory, and/or vibrotactile feedback. This is followed by a cognitive processing of the information. The third stage is the selection of a response or the making of an appropriate decision. The fourth stage involves the repositioning of the hands and feet on the steering wheel and pedals, thus re-establishing control. The fifth and final stage is the actual reaction, which may take the form of steering and braking with sufficient force [6–10].
1.2 Effects of non-driving-related tasks on takeover performance
Non-driving-related-tasks (NDRTs), which include activities such as listening to music, reading and texting, are categorised as tertiary tasks. A substantial body of research has indicated heightened levels of engagement in non-driving-related tasks during autonomous driving mode [11, 12]. The impact of non-driving activities on performance during takeover is examined (e.g. [13, 14]), and the effect of extended autonomous driving on the driver’s ability to take over is studied (e.g. [15]). The extant literature reports an increase in workload caused by non-driving activities and a decrease in situational awareness. Thus, drivers may need a longer time to reorient themselves to the driving task [11]. In the event of a non-driving-related task interfering with the takeover, the resulting impact on the speed and quality thereof is significant. A deterioration of all monitored parameters is usually observed, including an increase in reaction time, a decrease in time-to-collision (TTC), and an increase in the number of collisions [16, 17]. Conversely, Shahini & Zahabi [8] found that driving without non-driving-related tasks leads to better performance, specifically in terms of shorter hands-on-wheel
