time, shorter braking time, longer TTC, and fewer accidents compared to driving with a non-driving activity. Moreover, drivers who performed non-driving related tasks exhibited a significantly higher takeover time compared to those who did not engage in such activities [1821]. In addition, these drivers demonstrated a decline in driving performance [22, 23]. The assessment of non-driving-related tasks is typically based on characteristics that have the potential to impede the driver’s capacity to execute the requisite steps necessary for the transition (e.g., recording a signal to take over, interrupting a non-driving activity, etc.). The demands that NDRTs place on the driver correspond to specific limitations in the ability to take control, based on which the individual dimensions of these activities can be defined [24]. A particularly salient feature pertains to the sensory modality of the activity. Numerous studies have demonstrated that the sensory modality of an activity exerts a substantial influence on the capacity to regain control [25–27]. The primary distinctions can be delineated between auditory and visual activities auditory activities exhibit a diminished negative impact on driving performance when re-establishing control in comparison to visual activities. Furthermore, Zhang et al. [9] demonstrated that engaging in visual-auditory and visual-motor activities increased the time required to regain control, while no effect was observed with auditory-cognitive activities. The influence of the modality is increased by the further characteristics. Visual activities, when accompanied by a higher mental load, have been observed to result in the most significant deterioration in driving performance [13, 25–28]. Concentrating on the concept of visual attention has been shown to be advantageous [29]. The combination of visual modality and manual load while holding an object in the hand also significantly impairs driving performance during a takeover [25–27, 30, 31]. In the context of auditory activities, extant research has demonstrated that activities necessitating speech input or auditory resources result in enhanced driving performance with respect to lateral and longitudinal control of the vehicle, in addition to sufficient visual evaluation of the road when compared to visually and manually demanding tasks [26, 27]. Furthermore, research findings indicate that the duration of takeover time is increased when drivers engage in activities involving handheld devices or objects. The act of holding an object and then releasing it has been demonstrated to result in a prolonged duration required to regain control [9, 31, 32]. This has been shown to lead to an augmentation in reaction time, for instance, when individuals place their hands on the steering wheel [26, 33]. Conversely, the impact of operating integrated electronic devices (i.e. without holding them) on the process of taking control has been examined. The findings of this
study suggest that the operation of integrated electronic devices does not exert a substantial negative impact on performance [8]. However, concurrently, an escalating level of mental and visual load has been demonstrated to result in a decline in takeover performance [9]. Another significant criterion for the classification of activities is the degree of cognitive or visual load (demands of the activity). A substantial body of research has demonstrated that all types of loads have the capacity to exert a detrimental effect on an individual’s ability to take over the wheel, thereby impacting driving performance (see [28, 32]). Visual load has been identified as a particularly salient factor in this regard. The elevated level of load has been demonstrated to result in an escalation in the driver’s mental load [13, 20, 32, 34], which has been shown to precipitate a decline in performance during the takeover, particularly in circumstances where the prevailing traffic conditions are characterised by heightened demands and complexity [31]. When the driver turns his attention to NDRTs, his situational awareness decreases because his limited attentional resources are no longer used to monitor the car and the overall traffic situation [12]. If the activities are more demanding, the increased mental load prevents the driver from developing sufficient situational awareness, which secondarily leads to a poor assessment of the situation and erroneous reaction [23, 35, 36].
2 Method
2.1 Participants
A total of 35 participants – professional drivers took part in the experiment on the truck driving simulator from September to December 2023. The final sample for the purposes of all subsequent analyses comprised 28 male and 3 female participants. The data of 4 participants was excluded due to symptoms of simulator sickness. The mean age of the participants was 40 years (range: 23–57 years). The plurality of participants reported between 11 and 20 years of experience (32.3%, n = 10). Furthermore, the plurality of participants (35.5%; N = 11) drove between 40,001 and 60,000 km per year.
2.2 Ethical approval
The ethical process was ensured through a code of ethics, which is guaranteed by the ethics committee of the research institution CDV – Transport Research Centre (Líšeňská 33a, 636 00 Brno, Czechia). The study was approved by this committee. The participants were informed about the potential risks associated with the experiment and were given the option to withdraw from the study at any time. Informed consent was obtained from all participants prior to the commencement of the experiment. The experiment was overseen by an


