instructor to ensure the safety of the participants. Participants were financially rewarded.
2.3 Apparatus
The primary measurement was conducted on a truck and bus simulator. The system is a complex one, consisting of several elements: an original Volvo truck cab (which was adapted for performing NDRTs), a motion platform simulating physical forces during vehicle driving, a visual system representing the driver’s field of vision, an operator centre, a PC, and SimWorld Software. This high-fidelity moving base simulator represents the road and traffic environment, which is projected on screens in front of the vehicle with a broad field of view. Two screens positioned behind the vehicle display the scenery for the rearview mirrors. The driving performance following the takeover is recorded by the simulator software, including parameters such as the takeover time, speed, braking, and distance to the centreline (See Fig. 2).
2.4 Questionnaires
All participants completed questionnaires concerning their demographics, driving history, and frequency of performing activities in real life. A range of additional methods were employed, including the SAGAT method (Situational Awareness Global Assessment Technique) [23, 37], the NASA-TLX questionnaire (mental workload), and the Acceptance questionnaire of AV [38]. The participants were observed during their driving, and the view into the vehicle cabin was recorded. Following the conclusion of the experiment, the participants were invited to evaluate the perceived impact of the activities on takeover performance.
2.5 Experimental design, driving scenario and TOR 2.5.1 Experimental design
Each participant attended three sessions. The first session was an introduction to the vehicle control practice and instructions for taking over control was given, with a duration of five minutes. The second session involved a non-task session (baseline), during which the driving situation was observed in autonomous mode, with the session always concluding with the participant taking control and performing manoeuvres A, B, and C through three types of scenarios. The third session was a tasks session (experimental driving), during which six non-driving-related tasks were performed during autonomous driving and the participant took over driving. The duration of this session was 60 minutes.
2.5.2 Scenario
The autonomous vehicle was travelling on the motorway (favourable weather conditions and medium traffic density) in the right lane at a speed of 80 km/h. The driver was engaged in a non-driving-related task when an unexpected notification was received, prompting the initiation of manual driving. The signal was consistently generated approximately 10 seconds prior to the occurrence described below, i.e., approximately 220 metres before the driver was required to take control of the vehicle at the latest. The experiment is illustrated graphically in Fig.3 below.
2.5.3 Takeover request (TOR)
The drivers were informed that the primary objective was to execute a safe driving manoeuvre, with the speed of the reaction being of secondary importance, provided it occurred within 10 seconds. Participants were provided with a set of guidelines to facilitate the desired response. These guidelines are outlined below: 1. Sound the signal (maximum duration of 10 seconds), 2. Orient oneself within the situation, 3. Announce the takeover by pressing the horn, 4. Drive manually. Participants declared taking control by pressing the takeover button (on the steering wheel), which switched the vehicle from autonomous mode to manual vehicle control. The takeover scenario consisted of three types of nonemergency situations. The situation leading to the need to take over manual control was defined so that one of three manoeuvres was correct during manual control: A - continue smoothly in the right narrowed lane, B - avoid the obstacle in the right lane, i.e. turn into the left lane, while not endangering or restricting the vehicles driving there, C - safely bring the vehicle to a complete stop in the right-hand lane and create a rescue lane for the convoy of vehicles.
