2.6 Non-driving-related tasks
Each of the six non-driving-related tasks was performed for a duration of 10 minutes, thereby ensuring that the driver was adequately immersed in the activity (see also [5, 20, 39]. Thereafter, the takeover signal was obtained. The instruction to perform the activity to the best of their ability was given, since their performance would be evaluated (working on a laptop) or because they would be asked about the presented content (watching a video and audio course). The sequence and concise description of the tasks are outlined in Table 1:
2.7 Data measurement and processing 2.7.1 Objective measures
To ascertain the driver’s takeover performance, the following parameters were recorded: takeover time, braking and acceleration, speed, and position in the lane. The term “takeover time” refers to the interval that begins when the signal is received and ends when the button to engage manual vehicle control is pressed. Lateral control was measured by determining the position of the vehicle in relation to the lane’s centre line. This variable describes the distance to the centre of the lane of the vehicle during the takeover manoeuvre. The standard deviation of the vehicle’s lateral position (SDLP) is a related variable. The SDLP has been shown to provide insight into a driver’s ability to maintain the position during the manoeuvre [40]. In the context of longitudinal control, the focus shifted to the vehicle’s speed. It is hypothesised that smaller deviations in lateral position and longitudinal
speed indicate more consistent and seamless control [10]. All parameters were measured in both non-task sessions (baseline) and task sessions (with the influence of activity).
2.7.2 Self-reported measures
Several self-reported measures were evaluated. Situational awareness, measured by the SAGAT method (Situational Awareness Global Assessment Technique) [23, 37], was identified as the most significant factor. Following each takeover, the ride was halted, and the participant was debriefed about any objects, pedestrians, or traffic signs they had observed in the scene. This process enabled the determination of the activity’s impact on situational awareness and its subsequent deterioration.
2.8 Data analysis
Each participant provided data from self-reported questionnaires and experimental data from the simulator drive (baseline without NDRT and six NDRTs). As three different scenarios were tested, we need to compare the results that are aligned to the same situations. The second objective was to compare the influence of the activities with each other. The statistical analysis was conducted using SPSS 24, with descriptive statistics, means and the Wilcoxon Signed-Rank test, a non-parametric alternative to the paired t-test, employed at a significance level of 0.05.
