3 Results
3.1 Takeover time
The measurement of the takeover time was determined by the interval between the signal for the takeover being sounded and the pressing of the takeover button. The average time required to regain control without performing the activity was 2.8 seconds (M1 = 2.97; SD = 0.91; M2 = 2.58; SD = 0.92; M3 = 2.61; SD = 1.48). For activities, the average time taken was 3.8 seconds. The average times for these activities are presented in Table 2. For each group of activities leading to the same scenario (marked with the same symbol), the data for driving with two types of activities and driving without activity (baseline) are always shown. The shortest recorded takeover time was for the audio course (M = 2.97; SD = 1.25), while the longest was for the manual task (M = 4.81; SD = 1.94). Where significant, the difference between the baseline and task session takeover times can be attributed to the influence of activities, namely relaxation (p = 0.001), working on a laptop (p = 0.000), manual tasks (0.000), and watching a video (0.004). Conversely, activities such as making a phone call (p = 0.545) and participating in an audio course (p = 0.057) did not result in a significant prolongation of the average takeover time.
3.2 Lateral and longitudinal changes in takeover performance
The average speed was calculated within a time interval of 10 seconds after the takeover. The results for individual activities within the same scenario are presented in Table 3. For each activity group leading to the same scenario (same symbol), data for driving with two activity types and without activity (baseline) are shown. Statistically significant differences between the average speed in the baseline and during driving while performing an activity were demonstrated for several activities, namely relaxation (p = 0.002), working on a laptop (p = 0.000), audio course (p = 0.000), manual task (p = 0.002), and watching a video (p = 0.009). Table 4 contains data on the distance of the vehicle from the centre of the lane during driving after taking control. Each scenario group (same symbol) includes data for driving with two activity types and a driving without activity (baseline). The mean values and standard deviations (a measure of variability) were considered. Statistically significant differences in distance from the centre of the lane between the baseline and driving while performing an activity were not demonstrated for any activity.
