4 Discussion
The present study investigated the influence of NDRTs on takeover performance following a TOR. The findings indicated that all forms of NDRTs adversely affected driving performance, though the extent of this impact varied. The experimental results demonstrated that variations in reaction time can be attributed to the impact of the activity. The range of takeover time was from 2.53 to 2.97 seconds in the absence of any tasks, and from 3.06 to 4.81 seconds when tasks were performed. Statistically significant differences were identified for various activities, including relaxation, working on a laptop, and watching a video. The findings are consistent with those of other studies. Eriksson & Stanton [18] also refer to an average takeover time of 2.97 seconds. Petermann-Stock et al. [41] report a takeover time of 3.2 seconds. A metaanalysis by Soares et al. [42] of 36 studies revealed a range of reaction times between 1.5 and 3.5 seconds, based on various experimental conditions. Müller [40] examined takeover time as the duration from the presentation of the TOR to the application of a steering angle of more than 3° or pressing the brake by more than 10%. This time ranged from 1.10 to 1.64 seconds, with the longest times observed for watching videos, texting, and reading. The findings of this study corroborate the notion that tasks characterised by high levels of cognitive load and those involving visual stimuli can harm the temporal dynamics of the aforementioned processes. The present study posits that an elongated takeover time, in conjunction with variations in the activities, signifies a diminished driver response, attributable to the attributes of the activity in question, including manual, cognitive, or visual demands. These characteristics contribute to an increased latency, especially when compared to the baseline. In summary, manual tasks, laptop work, and video watching have a detrimental effect on the takeover time. Laptop work is an activity with high visual and mental load, and the computer is handheld, which causes deterioration of driving performance [9, 13, 28]. This activity requires significant cognitive and visual attention, making it harder for participants to shift focus and respond quickly to the signal. This activity likely imposes a considerable cognitive load, thereby reducing reaction speed. Furthermore, Wandtner et al. [26] posit that watching a video is a visual activity with a negative effect. The results of the study demonstrated a performance impairment related to the holding of an object in hand, as indicated by increased reaction time [9, 31, 32]. Manual activities also resulted in longer takeover times [9, 31, 32]. The influence in the presented
study is considered high, with the possibility of impairment by fatigue given that it was the final activity. The need for physical manipulation during the task is likely to distract from the primary task of driving, making it difficult for participants to react promptly. The telephone call exhibited a medium level of influence on the duration of the takeover. Notwithstanding the possibility of cognitive distraction, phone calls enabled a rapid takeover. This phenomenon may be attributable to various factors, including the participants’ optimal preparation at the commencement of the test, given that phone calls constituted the second activity, or the presence of unoccupied hands during the task. The relatively swift reaction observed indicates that, while phone calls do induce a degree of distraction, they do not significantly impede the ability to regain control within the temporal context of the task. The favourable outcome may be attributed to the practice effect associated with performing this activity in everyday life. The shortest observed takeover time was during relaxation. The enhanced responsiveness is likely attributable to the unobstructed hands and the restful state. This finding suggests that relaxation activities may facilitate a heightened state of readiness to regain control. Under the findings of other studies [26, 28], it is concluded that activities devoid of visual components (e.g., phone call, relaxation, audio course) result in more efficient takeover time in comparison to activities incorporating visual components. It is important to note that a quick reaction (i.e., a short takeover time) does not necessarily indicate a high-quality response. To assess the quality of the response, qualitative parameters such as driving speed must be taken into consideration. Speed is a critical factor that reflects safe versus risky behaviour after taking control of the vehicle. It is generally accepted that driving at a reduced speed and maintaining adequate braking are indicative of a safer driving style. Conversely, behaviours associated with increased speed and diminished braking are commonly linked to a less safe takeover. The speed of passing through situations is partially related to the type of scenario that the participants dealt with. To this end, the baseline of driving without activity is also provided, and the results are compared as paired samples. The study found that there were usually similar trends in speed changes between pairs of activities within the same scenarios. It is evident that participants consistently adopt a slower pace after engaging in the activity, a phenomenon that can be attributed to the tendency to decelerate to achieve an adequate level of situational awareness. The distance from the centre lane has been utilised in numerous studies (see [28, 40]). It is widely accepted that greater variability in the distance from the centre line generally indicates poorer vehicle control. In critical cases, the vehicle may “weave,” causing varying distances


