from the centre. In order to ensure safe vehicle control, it is necessary to establish a stable driving path and reduce vehicle oscillation. Research has shown that variables such as phone calls, driver relaxation, and the watching of videos have been found to be significant contributors to increased variability in driving path, thereby indicating suboptimal takeover conditions. Another significant variable in the context of takeover quality is situational awareness. The findings of this study demonstrated a significant decline in situational awareness during periods of phone usage. The present study, therefore, in accordance with international research, focuses on the impact of different types of activities or their characteristics. However, in contrast to numerous foreign studies, the present experimental study is distinguished by its examination of six non-driving-related tasks of varying types and characteristics. In contrast to the majority of foreign studies, which have focused on two to three non-drivingrelated tasks (e.g. [14, 20, 33], only a minority of studies have examined six or more activities [5, 23]. The incorporation of a more extensive array of activities has yielded novel insights into the impact of diverse species and the disparities between them. An augmented array of tasks permitted greater variability in the presented scenarios. Furthermore, the study enabled the observation of the impact of fatigue on takeover performance. A comprehensive review of extant studies reveals that the present study is among a select few that have measured multiple activities on a truck simulator with a motion platform. The majority of extant studies have focused on passenger vehicle simulators. A notable exception is the study by Zhang et al. [10], which investigated the differences in taking over the wheel between driving with system monitoring, without system monitoring while working on a tablet, and in a situation with eyes closed. The results obtained from this study indicated that total takeover times were significantly longer in driving without system monitoring, and there was a high degree of variability in both the driver-not-monitoring and eyes-closed conditions, in comparison to the driver-monitoring condition. The results suggest that a personalised driver readiness predictor is an important input parameter for a safe and comfortable transition of control [10]. This finding is consistent with the results obtained in this study – when monitoring the system exclusively, the takeover time is shorter compared to a situation where drivers are engaged in other activities.
4.1 Limitations
The experiment was conducted on a Volvo truck simulator, which differs in terms of ergonomics and controls from future L3/L4 autonomous vehicles. The research sample consisted of experienced professionals. It is hypothesised that the results obtained from this
professional sample will differ from those obtained from non-professional drivers who have less experience and driving skills. The experimental design involved driving on a motorway and creating several situations for the driver to react to. Situations representing regular traffic were deliberately chosen, rather than collision or risk situations that are often simulated in other studies. However, this approach has the disadvantage of not observing erroneous responses in the form of early non-takeover and subsequent threatened or realised collisions (TTC, collisions, etc.), but only trends in takeover rates, and lateral and longitudinal changes.
4.2 Implications for the praxis
It is necessary to limit the performance of some risky types of activities, ideally through legislative measures. It is also advisable to inform drivers about the process of taking over control and the impact of different types of activities. Furthermore, it has been demonstrated that it is of paramount importance to provide drivers with comprehensive support throughout the process of taking over the wheel. This necessitates ensuring that drivers are well-informed about the process and can engage in it. The vehicles themselves will also play an important role, e.g., through well-designed dashboards and infotainment settings. In the context of driver education, the utilisation of checklists to support driver readiness for operating an autonomous vehicle or during the process of taking over control is a recommended practice.
5 Conclusions
Engagement in NDRTs has been demonstrated to increase takeover time, whilst concomitantly resulting in a deterioration of qualitative parameters. Activities requiring manual dexterity, such as those involving handheld objects, result in prolonged takeover times when compared to activities that do not require such dexterity. Visual distraction has been identified as a significant factor in this regard. Mental workload has been shown to prolong takeover time and deteriorate other parameters. The present study has identified that for L3, the least appropriate tasks are those that are highly demanding, contain a visual modality, and include handholding. The majority of activities studied were inappropriate for L3. The audio course has been identified as the only viable option. A comprehensive analysis encompassing all measured parameters and respondents’ perspectives reveals that working on a laptop exerts the most detrimental influence (visual activity, elevated mental demands, handholding). A similar negative effect was observed regarding watching a video, which, although it involves visual activity and medium mental demands, does not involve handholding. Another activity that has been shown to have a negative impact is making a phone


