call (auditory activity, medium mental demands, no handholding). The influence of making a call was particularly evident in the qualitative aspects of the subsequent drive, while reaction time was, on the other hand, relatively short. However, respondents perceived this activity as relatively safe. Conversely, the manual activity (visual activity, low mental load, handholding) exhibited high latency, primarily attributable to object manipulation, resulting in prolonged reaction times. However, this activity also demonstrated a positive effect, with drivers being appropriately activated and ready to react. The relaxation activity (auditory activity, low mental load, no handholding) demonstrated both a negative and a positive influence. The positive effect was characterised by the subjects’ subjective experience of relaxation or refreshment, which manifested as reduced reaction times. In terms of the qualitative aspects of driving, this activity shows a certain degree of riskiness due to the ‘eyes closed’ factor, which may cause a decline in situational awareness. Conversely, the least risky activity was found to be listening to an audio course, which is considered to be the least risky activity. This auditory activity demonstrated favourable outcomes across the majority of the parameters under study.
